## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical implementation of per-tenant isolation in a shared GCP project, focusing on real-world controls rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- IAM Conditions and least-privilege service accounts
- VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect
- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)
- CI/CD gates with synthetic tenants and rollback safety
- Data Loss Prevention (DLP) and ongoing IAM reviews

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo snippet: enforce tenant-scoped sink bindings
const sink = getSink("bigquery-export");
bindPolicy(sink, {
  condition: {
    title: "tenant_scope",
    expression: 'resource.name.startsWith("projects/PROJ/datasets/tenant-")'
  },
  bindings: [{ role: "roles/bigquery.dataEditor", members: ["serviceAccount:tenant-a-sa@proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com"] }]
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the controls for a new tenant with stricter data residency requirements?
- How would you measure and report control effectiveness to audits?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design per-tenant secrets strategy, runtime access flow, rotation, and CI safety beyond data isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Secrets Manager per-tenant isolation
- Workload Identity for runtime retrieval
- CMEK-backed encryption and rotation
- CI gate validating cross-tenant access and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
const {SecretManagerServiceClient} = require('@google-cloud/secret-manager');
const client = new SecretManagerServiceClient();
async function accessSecret(tenantId, suffix) {
  const name = `projects/${PROJECT_ID}/secrets/tenant-${tenantId}-${suffix}/versions/latest`;
  const [version] = await client.accessSecretVersion({name});
  return version.payload.data.toString('utf8');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rotation impact on live traffic?
- How would you revoke access for a suspended tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses practical knowledge of API-layer tenant isolation using common GCP primitives. It tests understanding of JWT validation, IAM Conditions, quotas, logging, and CI validation—all essential skills for building secure multi-tenant SaaS applications.

## Key Concepts
- JWT validation and tenant_id extraction from tokens
- API Gateway configuration with per-tenant quotas and IAM Conditions
- Tenant-specific error handling and observability
- CI gate for synthetic tenants and safe rollback procedures

## Sample Implementation
```javascript
// Verify tenant_id from JWT claims
const verifyTenantAccess = (req, res, next) => {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;
  const token = authHeader?.split(' ')[1];

  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    if (!decoded.tenant_id) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Missing tenant_id claim' });
    }
    req.tenant_id = decoded.tenant_id;
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
  }
};

// Per-tenant quota check
const checkQuota = async (tenantId) => {
  const limit = await quotaService.getLimit(tenantId);
  const used = await quotaService.getUsage(tenantId);
  if (used >= limit) {
    throw new Error('Quota exceeded');
  }
};

// Tenant-scoped logging
const logWithTenant = (tenantId, action, metadata) => {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    tenant_id: tenantId,
    action,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    ...metadata
  }));
};
```
